[Music]  you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news coming up  a new tool to map plastic waste from  space can it help government solve the  pollution problem  an energy company boss calls for the  government to step in before rocketing  prices see millions fall into fuel  poverty  and are music lovers more likely to  commit to climate action than the rest  of us  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes happening to our world hear from  the people being hit hardest by those  changes and reports on the race for  solutions so new satellite technology  has allowed scientists to map plastic  waste from space for the first time  global plastic watch can detect plastic  sites as small as five by five meters in  near real time and much of this waste  ends up in the ocean so just how bad is  plastic pollution well it's estimated  that 8 million tons of plastic enters  our oceans every year that's on top of  150 million tons of plastic that are  already circulating our marine  environments and as a consequence  plastic has been found in more than  sixty percent of sea birds and in  hundred percent of sea turtles who often  mistake plastics for food so  could tracking plastic pollution using  this new satellite imagery tool freely  available to the public to help solve  the problem either bennett reports  it is a blight on our planet that is  only getting worse the equivalent of one  truckload of plastic waste enters the  world's oceans every minute but now for  the first time our plastic pollution can  be mapped from space in what's being  seen as a potentially game-changing  breakthrough plastic pollution may  result in  in health issues both uh to ecosystem as  well to human that we don't quite  understand yet um and so i think it's  exceptionally important that we tackle  uh this growing issue  global plastic watch uses satellite  imagery and artificial intelligence to  map plastic pollution on land in near  real time it can detect sites as small  as five by five meters 26 countries have  been mapped so far with the aim of  preventing plastic from flowing into the  oceans  each of these yellow dots represents a  plastic dumping site and if you click on  one  you can see what it looks like from  space now because it uses satellite  imagery that's updated fairly regularly  you can track how the size of the site  has changed over time that's the yellow  pattern here  and if you click on street view  then you can see what it looks like  on the ground  but how can they be sure the rubbish on  land ends up in the sea in some cases so  uh uh it is very apparent from the high  resolution satellite imagery so you can  almost just see it like waste gets piled  up and then a monsoon comes by and it  strips out all the waste from behind  the free technology is already being  used by indonesia the fifth highest  contributor to ocean plastics but  developers say it's not about naming and  shaming rather empowering governments to  stem the tide iva bennett sky news  well on the eve of the queen's speech  the head of one of the country's biggest  energy suppliers has called for the  government to be more proactive in  easing costs for consumers the head of  scottish power says 10 million  households should have their energy  bills reduced by a thousand pounds to  avoid falling into energy poverty but  the government says they'll do nothing  more before the autumn helen ann smith  is our business correspondent  it's a pretty stark intervention we've  seen from the ceo of scottish power he  is calling on the government to do much  more to help people with the rising  costs of energy bills he wants a  thousand pounds taken off people's bills  that he says can be paid for over 10  years either by putting small amounts on  people's bills going forward or through  taxation he essentially makes the point  that energy bills are already some of  the highest they've ever been the energy  price cap went up to 2000 pounds a year  in april that is set to go up again  because remember that price cap was set  before the invasion of ukraine which has  sent wholesale gas prices soaring even  higher than what they were so it's  expected that come october that price  cap could rise to as much as 2 500  pounds a year maybe even up to 3 000.  and his point uh keith anderson's point  was that that's just going to be simply  unaffordable for people we're a big  company and we can survive and we can  get through this okay but what we're  saying is with the rises we're seeing  and the price going to that level we  can't do this on our own so i can't help  customer customers on my own deal with  this price and deal with in effect for  over a period of 18 months would be a  200 increase in their energy bill that  requires the government the regulator  and the industry all to work together to  help sort this out now the timing of  this intervention is very salient it is  just after the local elections where the  conservative government has taken a real  hammering in the polls and the key issue  it took a hammering over was the cost of  living but also it's the day before the  queen's speech and we are expecting an  announcement on the uk's energy strategy  the long-term solution to this  is more energy security whereby we are  in control of our own energy prices we  are not beholden to russian gas and the  wholesale gas price uh that even though  we don't buy a lot of russian gas we are  beholden to the price  across the market  so more  news tomorrow probably on nuclear and  renewable strategy things that the  government will hope means that things  like this won't happen in the future  well let's have a look at some of the  day's other climate news now because  plans for a uk-based mineral processing  plant that will supply enough lithium  for one million electric vehicles have  taken a step forward green lithium a  company that plans to build europe's  first lithium refinery in the uk has  struck a deal with commodities trading  giant trafagura at the facility in the  north of england will provide lithium to  gigafactories from 2024 as countries  increase their production of electric  vehicles in the quest for net zero  new measures to protect forest elephants  are being introduced in gabon where  playing a key role in conserving  rainforests now officials are set to  introduce eco-tourism sustainable  forestry and electric fences to keep  them critically endangered elephants  away from crops and humans the  government there says it's achieved near  zero deforestation and plans to increase  its protected areas from 20 22 to 30 by  2030.  the duke of sussex has taken well a  starring role in a video promoting a new  eco travel campaign travellers now the  non-profits organization has introduced  a rating tool on the website for new  zealand where holidaymakers rather than  holidays are rated for how sustainable  they are in the video prince harry is  confronted by kiwi actors playing the  role of  rating agents who rate him for his  environmental performance during his  2018 royal tour of new zealand  you scored three stars three stars out  of what 100 100 no out of five okay okay  funny uh so yeah not too bad according  to my notes here you were given 12  towels at tim's motor lodge and you only  used one for the entire stay so pretty  good i don't think i stayed there and 12  towels that's pretty excessive i mean 12  towels is excessive oh you were very  respectful of local communities in fact  you even bought some local honey yes i  remember that was that nice  uh  yes  now we're getting quite personal now but  bear with me if you listen to music say  in the shower or on your way to work or  maybe you've booked tickets for that  festival you just can't miss then you're  more likely to be more committed to  climate action than anyone else that's  what we're hearing today from a new  study called turn up the volume and  it also suggests music lovers believe  tackling climate change should be a  priority above other issues well lead  author of that study is dr matt brennan  associate professor of popular music at  the university of glasgow and he joins  me now matt thanks so much for joining  us and i suppose my first question is  that music culture has a  long history doesn't it playing a key  role in in pushing so social movements  this must be encouraging data for you  yeah i think that there's maybe a myth  actually that music was maybe more  linked to social movements in the 60s  and 70s but in recent decades maybe lost  some of that wider social resonance but  these findings really show us that the  link between music audiences and broader  social consciousness is still very  strong when it comes to concern about  climate change in particular  and matt you spoke to it's up to about 2  000 people  who are these music lovers that are  closely aligned with climate change what  did you learn from them  yeah we spoke to just over 2000 adults  across the uk and importantly they  weren't all music lovers the survey was  distributed by yougov to a broad range  of respondents and that's what allowed  us to make a comparison between fans and  non-fans so we asked people to rate how  important music was to them personally  and also in their daily routines and  from that we got a data set of about a  thousand fans for whom music was  important or very important and roughly  a thousand others who said music wasn't  so important to them and we could then  compare those two groups when it came to  their attitudes and behaviors on climate  change  one of the takeouts matt that i found  quite surprising is that 82 of music  fans say they're concerned about climate  change um but 54 of music lovers believe  tackling climate change should be top  priority above other issues um what do  you think the findings what do you think  you can do with those findings rather  and how can that be used to tackle  climate change in real terms  yeah i think we can use them in at least  two ways first we can use the findings  to persuade the music industry to take  even bolder steps towards reducing their  footprints ones that might even require  audiences to change their behavior a bit  because we now have data showing that  music audiences would support that more  strongly compared to the general public  and then second i think there's an  opportunity to engage music audiences  who feel more strongly about climate  change to help drive change maybe beyond  the music industry because music may not  have a very big carbon footprint  relative to other sectors of industry  but it does rely on that much bigger and  quite unglamorous infrastructure of high  emitting industries like transport  manufacturing construction and so on so  we can think about new ways maybe that  we might encourage music fans to adopt  sustainable behaviors as a way of  accelerating green transition across  that wider infrastructure and society  that they're already embedded in  thanks matt well do stay with us coming  up  i'll be joined by sally iran from forum  for the future and chaitanya kumar who's  head of environment at the new economics  foundation will be discussing politics  and culture have we reached a tipping  point in the climate conversation  [Music]  [Applause]  i'm david blevins and i'm skye's senior  ireland correspondent  [Music]  i'm based in belfast a city transformed  by peace but still struggling with its  past  politically the two sides are as far  apart as ever and it's over that  question of who has sovereignty in  northern ireland  the very issue that's been the source of  tension here for  generations it takes 60 minutes to cross  the irish sea it took the british  monarchy a century  i've spent 25 years reporting the  journey from conflict to peace  the political parties of northern  ireland have reached agreement  we help you understand the world with us  today  we have reached an agreement with the  conservative party on support for  government  in parliament  we already know what the government gets  out of this deal what exactly does the  dup get out of it well 1.5 billion  pounds  welcome to winterfell it's an iconic  location in this globally acclaimed  drama  ophelia was the most powerful hurricane  this far east in the atlantic on record  you can't live in a place like this and  not appreciate the environment  the plan is to take the political  temperature midweek so we'll be  discussing the biggest stories of the  week so far talking about what's still  to come having a look at prime  minister's questions of course and  getting reaction from politicians plus  our very own viewers and we'll have  analysis from my sky news colleagues the  take with sophie ridge on sky news  hello and welcome back to the daily  climate show on sky news well let's get  into some of those climate issues now  with our guests uh sally uran from the  forum for future and chaitanya kumar  thanks both for joining us today um i  just want to  start off with our first big topic  because um you would have heard last  week local elections the green party  made record gains didn't they uh they  now have over 540  council seats across the country and in  particular the party made history in  coventry as it took its first seat on  the council 50 years after the political  group was formed in the city so sally  let me start with you first of all  could you tell me why you think the  success of uh the greens has sort of  turned into this big story at the local  elections  yeah i think there are a number of  reasons i think the first is that we are  just hearing more about sustainability  and climate issues in mainstream media  and we're seeing more of climate change  issues as we go about making our  shopping choices when we're in the  supermarket when we're online so i think  this agenda has started to come into the  mainstream and this is coupled with the  fact that we're also seeing changes in  our climate the massive heat wave  reported in india last year last week  but also closer to home and this also  comes at a time when actually the green  party were quite specific about what you  could do as individuals in terms of  insulating your home reducing your  energy bill so in the face of a cost of  living right right there were some very  specific  guidelines there which i think probably  resonated with people at a time when we  really are struggling with cost of  living and i can see that you're nodding  your head chaitana are you in agreement  then about the success of the green  party being down to those key issues  like the cost of living  yeah i think so i think  some people on the political right  certainly wanted to see this as a almost  like competition between cost of living  and acting on climate change if you want  to solve one crisis you have to sort of  ignore the other but i think this  election was a further vindication of  the fact that we don't have to look at  them separate issues they are  interlinked and stacking climate change  and tackling that zero can have  implications for dealing with cost of  living and vice versa as well  i suppose climate action  um is something that lots of people are  thinking about and with the green party  in particular  you would take from this that it's not a  fringe party anymore people are not  protest voting they're not going to the  lib dems or going to labor they're  picking the green party as their main  political preference this time round of  course local elections are quite  different as sally mentioned earlier  point about insulating homes for example  we argue that when it comes to  insulating homes retrofitting homes  local authorities have a very important  role and therefore having green  councillors  leading your wards i think is a very  good sign but of course there's a slight  difference between national elections  and local elections as well it'll be  interesting to see how many candidates  at the national level perhaps get into  parliament in a couple of years time but  i think the it's a good sign that  climate change is very much of the  agenda and i wouldn't be surprised if  it's one of the top three issues leading  up to the elections in a couple of years  i'm glad you brought that up chaitanya  because sally i was going to ask you  what you think this says for the future  and a general election for the green  party  i think this is great news i think this  is an indication we might be at a  tipping point where the public  consciousness about this agenda is  really hitting how people are making  their political choices and choices  about how they live their lives and so i  agree i think we're going to see an  increasing spotlight shining on this  issue and it will be up there in the  political discourse because this affects  everybody and i think really why the  greens did so well is that often climate  can feel quite disempowering it can feel  quite hard to know how to navigate this  really complex agenda but simple  messages about what you can do at home  how you can build local communities  these are all things that we can action  and i think as we kind of bridge that  divide between the theory and reality of  how do we respond to climate then i  think we'll see this elevating up the  political discourse  well it feels like doesn't it sally that  climate action is in all stages of life  in particular in the media industry  because um did you guys watch the baftas  last night at all  i had to confess i did watch some of it  yes you did great because there was that  big moment wasn't there in terms of  climate action when the duke of  cambridge he praised television writers  for including environmental messages in  their shows to keep climate change in  the minds of viewers so uh prince  william if you didn't see it said  writers and producers are playing a  unique role in ensuring the future of  our planet is something that we all want  to talk about so sally he highlighted  the uh the bbc's big spelling bee that  was a really big one for him i'm sure  he'd very much welcome this show um he  also spoke about uh the north london  derby on sky sports where there was a  sustainability quiz between two top  football players and i think 2.6  million people watch that game in  particular  this relationship between the media  industry and environmental issues when  did you notice a turning point  i think probably in the last 12 to 18  months as we headed into cop26 for the  first time in my career i saw a really  serious conversation about the need to  have a mainstream discussion around  climate and i do think the media has an  incredibly important role to play if we  look about how big change happens and  certainly our response to climate needs  to be bridged within this big change  then behavior change narrative shift is  so important and changing the narrative  from a set of issues that people don't  necessarily understand or care about  actually this affects us all and this  something that we can all do is so  important so i was delighted to hear  that intervention last night and it's  really really timely we have seen more  broadcasters bring in these issues into  their mainstream broadcasting sky is a  great example  but one stat looked at scripts over the  last five years  of mainstream tv productions and less  than three percent talked about climate  and this is impacting us all and so i'd  love to see more from media because  shifting the narrative of this is an  agenda that will impact us all and we've  all got a role to play the future isn't  written  so chaitanya we were talking about  prince william there but prince harry  was also involved in pushing the climate  agenda  with his involvement in that video to  i guess rate holidaymakers rather than  holidays and give tips on you know  eating locally how important is it that  members of the royal family or big  celebrities get involved in trying to  push the agenda for climate change  yeah i think it's quite important i i  enjoyed the ad uh i giggled a bit it was  funny um but i think the celebrities  have a lot of pulling power you know  they have millions of people following  them on social media  every word they say obviously can  potentially become news and then talking  about climate change is obviously quite  important quite significant i suppose  the difference of course is there's a  risk that we may end up having  celebrities just talking about recycling  or like turn the lights off and things  like which are all important to mind you  but we need a lot more than that we need  governance acting and i think  celebrities can push the boundaries of  what  they can do politically as well and  perhaps you know let their followership  and and and fans  also perhaps be more politically active  and the other story that we were talking  about earlier on was about music and how  music lovers are more likely to be in  tune with climate issues um sally i  wonder are you a music fan do you think  this is true  i do um because music speaks to how we  express ourselves our creativity  and for all too often the conversation  around climate has been quite a  technical conversation  it's popped people off because it seems  quite hard to understand and the science  is really scary however if we can unlock  a creative discussion about how we can  work together so celebrities civil  society business government then  actually we reshift the narrative into  something that we all can grab hold of  and play a role in delivering  okay thank you sally and chaitanya thank  you so much for joining us today on  tomorrow's show i will be joined by  economist vicky price and climate  columnist at the independent donna chad  mccarthy well thanks for watching  everyone and we'll see you next time bye  [Music]  you

